WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:03.719
I’m sure you’ve all heard about random
college students and young adults turning

00:00:03.719 --> 00:00:08.250
a couple hundred or thousand dollars into
hundreds of thousand or even millions by investing

00:00:08.250 --> 00:00:10.080
into momentum cryptos.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:16.049
There was even some guy who turned $8000 into
$5.7 billion by investing into Shiba Inu.

00:00:16.049 --> 00:00:19.540
If you’ve made life changing amounts of
money with these altcoins congratulations,

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.380
that’s really quite awesome.

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.180
But, there’s someone who’s making a lot
more than all of these guys while taking on

00:00:25.180 --> 00:00:28.350
far less risk, and that’s of course the
crypto brokerages.

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:32.750
You know what they say, when there’s a gold
rush, you don’t wanna be digging for gold,

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.679
you wanna be selling the shovels, and that’s
exactly what Sam Bankman did.

00:00:36.679 --> 00:00:42.710
In May of 2019, Sam launched his crypto trading
platform FTX, and now he’s worth $26.5 billion.

00:00:42.710 --> 00:00:46.859
And did I mention that he's only 29 years
old.

00:00:46.859 --> 00:00:50.110
Forbes claims that Sam is actually the richest
person in crypto.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:54.960
Satoshi Nakamoto is of course richer, but
if we just look at known figures, Forbes is

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.070
probably right.

00:00:56.070 --> 00:01:03.210
So, here’s the story of Sam Bankman, and
how he became the richest known crypto figure.

00:01:03.210 --> 00:01:07.829
Taking a look back, Sam was born on March
6, 1992 in Stanford, California.

00:01:07.829 --> 00:01:11.750
Both of his parents were law professors at
Stanford, so I think it’s safe to assume

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:13.810
that Sam had a well off childhood.

00:01:13.810 --> 00:01:17.740
He spent a lot of his free time reading Harry
Potter and watching the San Francisco giants.

00:01:17.740 --> 00:01:22.170
As for school, he attended a small private
school in the bay before scoring a spot at

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:24.579
MIT where he majored in physics.

00:01:24.579 --> 00:01:29.079
Physics is one of the hardest majors to complete
if not the hardest, and Sam was doing it at

00:01:29.079 --> 00:01:32.650
MIT, so you would think that college consumed
all of his time.

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.950
But, Sam says that he was able to half ass
his way through it.

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:39.670
Apparently, he spent most of his time playing
video games including Starcraft and League

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:41.759
of Legends as opposed to studying.

00:01:41.759 --> 00:01:44.570
So, we’re probably looking at a child prodigy.

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:49.009
Initially, Sam was looking to become a physics
professor similar to his parents, but as he

00:01:49.009 --> 00:01:52.990
developed a strong interest in ethics and
morality, he slowly strayed away from this

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:53.990
path.

00:01:53.990 --> 00:01:57.760
Sam was especially interested in effective
altruism and utilitarianism.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.179
Instead of donating money to trendy causes,
Sam believes that one should donate money

00:02:01.179 --> 00:02:04.100
to causes that will objectively do the most
good.

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:07.820
For example, causes that save the most amount
of lives or create the most amount of income

00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:09.009
per dollar donated.

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:13.560
In order to make such an impact though, Sam
knew that he needed big money and he simply

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:16.060
wasn’t going to get there by being a professor.

00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:20.569
So, instead of pursuing higher level degrees,
Sam took on a lucrative finance job at a quantitative

00:02:20.569 --> 00:02:22.680
firm called Jane Street Capital.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.769
Unlike other crypto billionaires, Sam never
really cared about crypto and he wasn't an

00:02:26.769 --> 00:02:28.569
early adopter by any means.

00:02:28.569 --> 00:02:33.180
In fact, crypto didn’t catch his attention
till 2017 when Bitcoin was running from a

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.940
few thousand dollars to $20 thousand.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.500
Even then, he didn’t really care about the
fundamentals of Bitcoin or what it stood for.

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:44.159
He wasn’t interested in Bitcoin’s promise
of being an inflation hedge or it’s decentralized

00:02:44.159 --> 00:02:45.200
nature.

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.830
What he was interested in though was Bitcoin’s
inefficient markets.

00:02:48.830 --> 00:02:52.739
One day, Sam noticed that you could buy bitcoin
in the US and sell it on secondary markets

00:02:52.739 --> 00:02:54.879
for a substantially larger amount.

00:02:54.879 --> 00:02:56.530
And that’s how it all started.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:00.439
Sam says quote, “I got involved in crypto
without any idea what crypto was.

00:03:00.439 --> 00:03:03.510
It just seemed like there was a lot of good
trading to do.”

00:03:03.510 --> 00:03:07.100
Something else that attracted Sam to crypto
was the lack of regulation.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:10.830
Opening up a traditional trading firm requires
a lot of licenses and help from lawyers which

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:12.020
is not cheap.

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:16.810
But, with crypto, basically anyone could open
a trading firm especially if it wasn’t based

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:18.189
in the US.

00:03:18.189 --> 00:03:21.600
Sam wasn’t the only one trying to leverage
this opportunity though.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:24.900
Exploiting this price difference to make profits
is actually an established form of trading

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:26.750
called arbitrage trading.

00:03:26.750 --> 00:03:30.379
Anyway, Sam would end up quitting his job
and he would start an arbitrage crypto trading

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:33.849
firm called Alameda research in October of
2017.

00:03:33.849 --> 00:03:37.820
Initially, it was just him and a couple of
recent college graduates, but despite the

00:03:37.820 --> 00:03:41.870
rookie nature of their startup, it didn’t
take long for them to raise a lot of money.

00:03:41.870 --> 00:03:45.549
Given Sam’s experience working on Wall Street,
he was able to brand himself as the wall street

00:03:45.549 --> 00:03:48.319
professional that was offering crypto trading
services.

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:51.189
Combine this with the fact that the rest of
wall street didn’t want to associate with

00:03:51.189 --> 00:03:56.430
crypto at least not publicly, and the extreme
fomo associated with the 2017 bull run, and

00:03:56.430 --> 00:03:58.970
Sam was able to raise millions within a few
months.

00:03:58.970 --> 00:04:04.630
By January of 2018, Sam was trading $25 million
worth of Bitcoin per day, but this was the

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:08.680
peak of the Bitcoin bull run and things only
went downhill from there.

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:12.260
Sam wasn’t worried about the hype dying
off though as he had sensed a much larger

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:13.510
opportunity.

00:04:13.510 --> 00:04:16.800
As he was trading millions of dollars per
day, he noticed that existing crypto brokerages

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:18.870
were trash when it came to trading.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.570
The popular crypto brokerages like Coinbase
had spent all of their effort on making crypto

00:04:22.570 --> 00:04:26.100
purchases as seamless as possible for individual
investors.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:29.570
And while they were phenomenal at this, they
weren’t very good at handling high speed

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:31.190
trade volume from traders.

00:04:31.190 --> 00:04:36.330
So, Sam decided to create his own crypto brokerage
that would be tailored towards crypto traders.

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:39.990
Over the next several months, Sam became more
involved with the crypto community by attending

00:04:39.990 --> 00:04:43.550
various crypto conferences and meeting with
various crypto investors and traders from

00:04:43.550 --> 00:04:44.610
around the world.

00:04:44.610 --> 00:04:47.990
After attending a crypto conference in Macau
China, Sam would actually choose to move to

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:51.360
Hong Kong, and this is where he would launch
FTX.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:54.870
Sam took profits from Alameda and worked with
a couple of venture capital firms to raise

00:04:54.870 --> 00:05:00.220
a total of $8 million which he would use to
develop FTX throughout the beginning of 2019.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:04.330
With $8 million and all of his experience,
Sam was able to develop the perfect platform

00:05:04.330 --> 00:05:08.690
for traders, and he eagerly launched FTX in
May of 2019.

00:05:08.690 --> 00:05:12.930
But to Sam’s disappointment the launch of
FTX was nothing like the launch of Alameda.

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:16.259
Alameda was launched during the peak of the
bull run when many investors were desperate

00:05:16.259 --> 00:05:17.380
to get into crypto.

00:05:17.380 --> 00:05:23.981
But by may of 2019, Bitcoin had crashed 80%,
Ethereum had crashed 94% and there was little

00:05:23.981 --> 00:05:25.139
to no hype left.

00:05:25.139 --> 00:05:30.660
It was basically just the hodlers and bag
holders left and these guys had no interest

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:31.789
in trading.

00:05:31.789 --> 00:05:36.000
Sam even hired a dozen salesmen to stand around
at WeWork locations to try to convince traders

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:39.210
to give FTX a shot, but still no one was interested.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:41.590
So, Sam started to think outside of the box.

00:05:41.590 --> 00:05:45.539
Bitcoin no longer had hype, so he couldn’t
rely on momentum traders who rode the wave

00:05:45.539 --> 00:05:46.639
up and down.

00:05:46.639 --> 00:05:49.690
But, something that Bitcoin still very much
had was volatility.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:53.410
So, Sam decided to lean in on the volatility
by introducing crypto derivatives.

00:05:53.410 --> 00:05:57.720
If you’re not familiar with derivatives,
it’s basically just leveraged trading through

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:00.230
the use of options, futures, and margin.

00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:03.740
Now, I cannot understate how dangerous these
are.

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:06.590
Trading derivatives on stocks itself is super
dangerous.

00:06:06.590 --> 00:06:10.020
And you could argue that crypto itself is
more dangerous than stock derivatives.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:14.660
So, when you're trading derivatives on crypto,
you’re basically trading derivatives of

00:06:14.660 --> 00:06:16.040
derivatives.

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:19.610
To make things even worse, there’s very
little regulation in crypto trading, so the

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:21.880
leverage you can take is sky high.

00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:25.759
On stocks, you’d be hard pressed to find
1x leverage without using options.

00:06:25.759 --> 00:06:29.220
Meanwhile, FTX offers up to 20x leverage.

00:06:29.220 --> 00:06:33.710
This means that with $10,000 in cash, you
can control $200,000 worth of Bitcoin.

00:06:33.710 --> 00:06:39.650
If Bitcoin rises just 5% you double your money,
but the thing that people forget is that if

00:06:39.650 --> 00:06:42.229
bitcoin just drops 5%, you lose everything.

00:06:42.229 --> 00:06:45.970
I don’t think I need to explain why this
is extremely dangerous.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:50.130
Ideally, Sam was looking to attract professional
traders who knew the risk and had experience

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:51.440
with leverage.

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.220
But naturally, he ended up attracting a large
number of retail gamblers looking for a moonshot

00:06:56.220 --> 00:06:57.220
opportunity.

00:06:57.220 --> 00:06:58.370
Nonetheless, this was extremely lucrative.

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:05.160
By May of 2020, FTX had grown to 200,000 users
with daily trading volume of $1 billion.

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.440
And since then, things have just gone to the
moon.

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:13.330
As of October, FTX boasted a total of 2 million
users with daily trading volume of $11.5 billion.

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:18.789
Now, to put that in perspective, Coinbase
has 68 million users or 34 times FTX.

00:07:18.789 --> 00:07:23.699
Yet, they only average about $5 billion per
day in volume which works out to about $74

00:07:23.699 --> 00:07:25.180
per person per day.

00:07:25.180 --> 00:07:29.480
Meanwhile, FTX averages $5,750 per day per
person.

00:07:29.480 --> 00:07:37.980
Now, FTX only charges a fee of 0.005% to 0.07%
per transaction, but with $11.5 billion per

00:07:37.980 --> 00:07:42.590
day, we’re talking about somewhere between
half a million per day to 8 million per day.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:47.229
If we estimate in the middle and call it $4
million, we’re looking at about $1.5 billion

00:07:47.229 --> 00:07:48.639
in revenue per year.

00:07:48.639 --> 00:07:52.069
And given that the business has very little
overhead, Forbes estimates that their profit

00:07:52.069 --> 00:07:54.150
margin is about 50%.

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:58.970
Aside from crypto derivatives, FTX has also
introduced tokens of popular stocks like Apple.

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:02.159
This allows investors from underprivileged
areas to safely participate in the American

00:08:02.159 --> 00:08:03.259
stock market.

00:08:03.259 --> 00:08:08.250
FTX also introduced their own token which
now accounts for a large part of Sam’s wealth.

00:08:08.250 --> 00:08:11.699
Given all of this exposure to crypto, you
would think that Sam is a massive believer

00:08:11.699 --> 00:08:15.200
in crypto, but this is actually not the case.

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:19.789
Now, it’s not like Sam is calling for Bitcoin
to go to 0, but he’s also not calling for

00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:21.300
it to go to the moon.

00:08:21.300 --> 00:08:25.409
He didn’t enter the crypto markets based
on conviction, he entered based on opportunity

00:08:25.409 --> 00:08:27.430
and that’s the only reason he’s still
there.

00:08:27.430 --> 00:08:31.319
At this point, he’s just hoping that crypto
survives long enough for him to give away

00:08:31.319 --> 00:08:35.290
his wealth as the only thing Sam has conviction
in is giving away all his money.

00:08:35.290 --> 00:08:39.900
If that’s through crypto and FTX, great,
but if crypto goes down and something else

00:08:39.900 --> 00:08:43.590
emerges, he’s just as fine with switching
to that instead.

00:08:43.590 --> 00:08:47.270
Right now, he’s reinvesting all of his profits
back into the business given that the company

00:08:47.270 --> 00:08:49.260
is only 2.5 years old.

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:52.870
But even through that, he’s managed to donate
$25 million.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:56.690
Sam hopes that by riding out the crypto wave
and holding off to sell, he can donate over

00:08:56.690 --> 00:08:59.170
900 times that or about $22.5 billion.

00:08:59.170 --> 00:09:04.590
Now, I don’t want to take away from his
generosity, but I do think it should be noted

00:09:04.590 --> 00:09:09.780
that Sam’s donations so far haven’t necessarily
been as altruistic as he originally proposed.

00:09:09.780 --> 00:09:14.400
Last year, Sam donated $5.2 million to Joe
Biden’s campaign which made him the second

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:17.880
largest individual donor only beaten out by
Michael Bloomberg.

00:09:17.880 --> 00:09:21.100
And I think we can all agree that political
donations are some of the least effective

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.380
donations when it comes to altruistic motives.

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:27.210
So, hopefully, Sam does eventually focus on
the altruistic avenues that he originally

00:09:27.210 --> 00:09:29.920
described, but only time will tell.

00:09:29.920 --> 00:09:34.750
In the meantime, that’s how Sam Bankman
went from 0 to $26.5 billion in just four

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:35.750
years.

00:09:35.750 --> 00:09:38.830
Do you guys believe that Sam will actually
follow through and donate all his money.

00:09:38.830 --> 00:09:42.870
And if so, do you think his donations will
actually be as altruistic as he describes?

00:09:42.870 --> 00:09:44.480
Comment that down below.

00:09:44.480 --> 00:09:48.720
Also, drop a like if you wish you could make
$26.5 billion in 4 years.

